<chapter xml:id="chapter-ordinary-differential-equations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Ordinary Differential Equations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      An ordinary differential equation (ODE) is an equation that relates an
      unknown function of a single variable to one or more of its derivatives.
      ODEs are the central mathematical tool for modelling dynamic processes:
      population growth, electrical circuits, mechanical vibrations, heat
      transfer, and countless other phenomena in engineering and the sciences.
    </p>
    <p>
      This chapter develops systematic solution methods for the two most
      important classes of ODEs encountered in a first engineering course.
      Section 3-1 treats <em>first-order ODEs</em>: we study separable
      equations (solved by separating variables and integrating) and linear
      first-order equations (solved by variation of constants).
      Section 3-2 extends these ideas to <em>higher-order linear ODEs</em>,
      with constant coefficients, covering the characteristic equation,
      the superposition principle, and the method of undetermined coefficients.
    </p>
  </introduction>

